# Wrong bit checks in `FLASH_FlushCaches` (stm32wlxx_hal_driver)

**Repository:** STMicroelectronics/stm32wlxx-hal-driver
**File:** `Src/stm32wlxx_hal_flash_ex.c`
**Function:** `FLASH_FlushCaches()`

## Summary

In `FLASH_FlushCaches`, the code checks cache-enable bits with `== 1U`:

```c
if (READ_BIT(FLASH->ACR, FLASH_ACR_ICEN) == 1U) {
    __HAL_FLASH_INSTRUCTION_CACHE_DISABLE();
    __HAL_FLASH_INSTRUCTION_CACHE_RESET();
    __HAL_FLASH_INSTRUCTION_CACHE_ENABLE();
}
...
if (READ_BIT(FLASH->ACR, FLASH_ACR_DCEN) == 1U) {
    __HAL_FLASH_DATA_CACHE_DISABLE();
    __HAL_FLASH_DATA_CACHE_RESET();
    __HAL_FLASH_DATA_CACHE_ENABLE();
}
```

However, `FLASH_ACR_ICEN` and `FLASH_ACR_DCEN` are **bit masks** defined as `(0x1UL << 9)` and `(0x1UL << 10)` respectively. `READ_BIT(...)` returns either `0` or the **mask value** (e.g., `0x200`, `0x400`), so comparing the result to `1U` is always false. As a result, caches are never flushed even when enabled.

## Expected behavior

Use a non-zero test for these mask bits:

```c
if (READ_BIT(FLASH->ACR, FLASH_ACR_ICEN) != 0U) {
    __HAL_FLASH_INSTRUCTION_CACHE_DISABLE();
    __HAL_FLASH_INSTRUCTION_CACHE_RESET();
    __HAL_FLASH_INSTRUCTION_CACHE_ENABLE();
}

#ifndef CORE_CM0PLUS
if (READ_BIT(FLASH->ACR, FLASH_ACR_DCEN) != 0U) {
    __HAL_FLASH_DATA_CACHE_DISABLE();
    __HAL_FLASH_DATA_CACHE_RESET();
    __HAL_FLASH_DATA_CACHE_ENABLE();
}
#endif
```

## Why this matters

If the instruction/data caches remain unflushed after erase/program operations, software may execute or read stale content from Flash, causing hard-to-diagnose runtime issues.

## Environment

* Series: **STM32WLxx** (observed on STM32WLE5JCI6)
* Package: `stm32wlxx_hal_driver` (current `main`, Nov 2025)

## Additional notes

This pattern might exist in other STM32Cube HAL repositories—worth a quick grep for `== 1U` checks against mask-type bit defines.

## Suggested fix

Replace both comparisons with `!= 0U` as shown above.

